    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Bottom Line\n**Negotiate.** Do not accept the offer as-is, and do not walk away without countering.\n\nYou are in a position of maximum leverage: you were headhunted, you are happy in your current role, and you hold a peer Staff/Senior-level position (IC6) at Google\u2019s primary talent competitor (Meta). \n\nOn paper, the offer\u2019s 4-year annualized average ($552,000/yr) is only **~5% above your current Meta run-rate ($526,000)**, and the base salary ($235,000) is actually a **$5,000 pay cut** relative to your current base. Google's initial L6 PM offers leave significant room across all levers (base, equity, and sign-on).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Breakdown: Meta IC6 vs. Google L6 Offer\n\n| Component | Current Meta IC6 | Offered Google L6 | Analysis |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **Below current base** ($183k\u2013$271k posted band) |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | +$11,000 |\n| **Annualized Equity** | ~$250,000/yr | ~$270,000/yr | +$20,000/yr (on 4-yr average) |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | N/A | **$0** | Standard initial low-ball |\n| **Annualized TC (Avg)** | **~$526,000** | **~$552,000** | **Only +5% over current** |\n\n#### Understanding Google\u2019s Equity Vesting Mechanics\nGoogle typically vests new-hire Restricted Stock Units (GSUs) on a **front-loaded schedule** (standard US schedule is **38% Year 1 / 32% Year 2 / 20% Year 3 / 10% Year 4** or 33% / 33% / 22% / 12%):\n\n* **Year 1:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $410.4k stock = **~$692,400**\n* **Year 2:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $345.6k stock = **~$627,600**\n* **Year 3:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $216.0k stock = **~$498,000**\n* **Year 4:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $108.0k stock = **~$390,000**\n\n*Why this matters:* While Years 1 and 2 deliver high immediate cash flow, Years 3 and 4 drop sharply unless offset by aggressive annual refreshers. Because refreshers are performance-dependent and never guaranteed, you want a higher initial grant and a sign-on bonus to protect your multi-year floor.\n\n---\n\n### 3. How Good Is This Offer for Google L6 PM?\nFor an L6 PM in San Francisco/Bay Area:\n* **Base Salary ($235k):** Low-to-mid band. Google tops out around **$255k\u2013$270k** for strong L6 hires.\n* **Equity ($1.08M over 4 yrs):** Near median (median is ~$1.0M\u2013$1.1M). Top of L6 band reaches **$1.3M\u2013$1.5M**.\n* **Sign-On ($0):** Weak. Google regularly uses **$50,000 to $100,000+** sign-on bonuses at L6 to close candidates from Meta or other top-tier peers.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What Exactly to Ask For\n\nDeliver a counter-offer targeting top-of-band total compensation:\n\n1. **Base Salary:** Ask for **$255,000** (or at minimum $250,000).  \n   * *Rationale:* You cannot take a base salary cut from your current $240,000.\n2. **Equity (GSUs):** Ask for **$1,350,000 over 4 years** (~$337.5k/year annualized average).\n3. **Sign-On Bonus:** Ask for **$80,000 \u2013 $100,000**.  \n   * *Rationale:* Bridges the gap for leaving your upcoming $200k Meta vesting tranche and unvested retention value.\n\nIf Google meets you in the middle, a revised package will look roughly like:\n* **Base:** ~$250,000\n* **Bonus (20%):** ~$50,000\n* **Equity:** ~$1,200,000 ($300k/yr avg; ~$456k in Yr 1)\n* **Sign-On:** ~$60,000\n* **Year 1 Total Comp:** **~$816,000** (~$600k 4-year average).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Script / Email Template\n\nYou can adapt this response for your recruiter:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this offer together. I\u2019ve really enjoyed meeting the team, and the scope of the role is compelling.*\n> \n> *As mentioned earlier, I wasn\u2019t actively looking and I\u2019m very well-supported at Meta with strong momentum. Looking at the numbers, the base salary represents a step down from my current $240k base, and the overall 4-year average doesn't quite reflect the premium needed to leave a role where I\u2019m established and have significant unvested equity.*\n> \n> *To make this an easy decision and feel confident about making the transition, I\u2019m looking for:*\n> * *A base salary of **$255,000***\n> * *An equity grant of **$1,350,000** over 4 years*\n> * *A sign-on bonus of **$90,000** to help offset my upcoming vesting schedule at Meta*\n> \n> *If we can get to these numbers, I\u2019m ready to sign and begin onboarding with the team.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 6. Non-Compensation Factors to Keep in Mind\n* **Pacing & Decision-Making:** Meta is known for faster execution velocity and bottom-up PM agency; Google PM culture involves broader cross-functional consensus and committee-driven approvals.\n* **Promotion to L7:** Moving from L6 to L7 at Google is notoriously slow and requires massive cross-org alignment. Ensure you enter L6 at top-of-band comp so you are not reliant on a near-term promotion for comp growth.",
