    "message": {
     "content": "### The Verdict\n**Financially, this is a strong, top-quartile Google L6 PM offer.** \n\nGoogle structured their counter specifically to solve your $200,000 unvested Meta RSUs through an equity boost ($1.08M $\\rightarrow$ $1.28M) rather than a sign-on bonus. \n\nWhether you should take it now comes down to **career trajectory and cultural fit**, because the financial case to move is now legitimately compelling\u2014especially over the first two years.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Numbers: Google vs. Meta\n\nWith Google\u2019s standard front-loaded vesting schedule (38% / 32% / 20% / 10%), here is how your realized compensation looks:\n\n| Metric | Meta IC6 (Status Quo) | Google L6 (Revised Offer) | Net Difference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $0 |\n| **Bonus (Target)** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $48,000 (20%) | +$12,000/yr |\n| **Year 1 Total** | ~$526,000 | **~$774,400** (38% RSU: $486.4k) | **+$248,400** |\n| **Year 2 Total** | ~$526,000 | **~$697,600** (32% RSU: $409.6k) | **+$171,600** |\n| **Year 3 Total** | ~$526,000 | **~$544,000** (20% RSU: $256.0k) | +$18,000 |\n| **Year 4 Total** | ~$526,000 | **~$416,000** (10% RSU: $128.0k)* | -$110,000* |\n| **2-Year Realized**| **~$1.05M** | **~$1.47M** | **+$420,000 Net Gain** |\n| **4-Year Average** | **$526,000/yr** | **$608,000/yr** | **+$82,000/yr (+15.6%)** |\n\n*\\*Note on Years 3\u20134:* The drop in Year 3 and Year 4 assumes zero refreshers. Google annual refreshers vest across subsequent years to smooth out this cliff, but you will need standard-to-strong performance ratings to keep your run-rate above $600k by Year 4.\n\n#### Did Google offset your $200k Meta unvested stock?\n**Yes.** In Year 1 alone, you will earn **~$248,000 more** than at Meta. Over the first 24 months, you will pocket **~$420,000 more** in cash and liquid equity than staying at Meta.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Is There Any Money Left on the Table?\nAt **$1.28M RSU**, you are in the top ~15\u201320% of Google\u2019s L6 PM equity band (which typically maxes around $1.3M\u2013$1.4M for non-competing-offer hires). \n\nWhen Google says \"this is our best offer\" after bumping equity by $200,000 and matching base, they have likely hit the ceiling of the approval tier for this hiring loop. Pushing further without a competing active offer in hand risks stalling the process.\n\n---\n\n### 3. How to Decide: Take It or Walk Away?\n\nSince compensation is now solved in Google's favor for at least the next 2\u20133 years, base your decision on these three factors:\n\n#### Reasons to **ACCEPT**:\n1. **Financial Arbitrage:** You lock in a ~$420k premium over your first two years.\n2. **Resume Diversification:** Having both Meta IC6 and Google L6 on your resume establishes you as a proven leader across both major tech archetypes.\n3. **Pacing / Work-Life Balance:** If you find Meta\u2019s PSC (Performance Summary Cycle) intensity exhausting, Google PM culture is generally more sustainable and less prone to sudden re-org volatility.\n\n#### Reasons to **STAY AT META**:\n1. **You Have Momentum Toward IC7:** Moving from IC6 to IC7 at Meta is typically faster and more merit/impact-driven than L6 $\\rightarrow$ L7 at Google (which involves heavy cross-functional consensus and committee processes).\n2. **Product Agency:** Meta PMs typically have more direct autonomy and top-down product decision-making power compared to Google PMs, who spend significant time building cross-org alignment.\n3. **Refresher Predictability:** Meta\u2019s refresher culture has historically been more formulaic and aggressive than Google\u2019s.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **If you want the Google role:**  \n   Send a closing email accepting the offer gracefully without pushing further on comp:\n   > *\"Thanks for working through the numbers with the compensation committee. Given the adjustment to the equity grant and base, I am excited to accept the offer. Let\u2019s finalize the paperwork and discuss start dates.\"*\n\n2. **If you want to use this to test Meta's appetite to retain you:**  \n   If you are truly split, you can quietly approach your Meta VP / Director:  \n   * *\"I was headhunted by Google for an L6 PM role. I wasn't looking, but they've put together a package paying ~$775k in Year 1 / ~$610k average. I love my team here, but the compensation delta is substantial. Is there any room for an out-of-cycle retention grant or accelerated refresher to help close this gap?\"*  \n   *(Only do this if your performance rating at Meta is consistently Exceeds/Redefines).*\n\n3. **If you genuinely love your current Meta role and dread changing teams:**  \n   Walk away knowing you successfully forced Google to their band ceiling, and you can re-engage them in 1\u20132 years if your situation at Meta changes.",
